## 2. Financial analysis

### Revenue and Profit Trends

| Indicators | FY2023 | FY2024 | FY2025 | FY2026 (first two quarters) |
|------|--------|--------|--------|------------------|
| Revenue | ~7 billion | ~7 billion | $7.1 billion | Down approximately 10% (year-over-year) |
| Net profit | Lower (impact of cyber attack) | US$280 million | US$810 million (+190%) | Down 18% year-on-year |
| Net profit margin | Low | 3.9% | 11.4% | Downward pressure |

**Note**: There is a one-time factor in the strong performance in FY2025. About 85-95 cents of earnings per share come from retailers' advance stocking before the ERP system switch (channel stuffing effect), and this part of the revenue will be regurgitated in FY2026.

### Key Financial Indicators

| Indicators | FY2024 | FY2025 | Description |
|------|--------|--------|------|
| Gross profit margin | 43.0% | 45.2% | Improvement 220 basis points |
| Net profit margin | 3.9% | 11.4% | Increased by 7.5 percentage points, but including one-time factors |
| ROE | 85.3% | 252.3% | The extremely high ROE is mainly due to high leverage (D/E 8.97), not pure operating improvement |
| Free cash flow | 483 million | 761 million | 10.7% of revenue |
| Dividend payment | 595 million | 602 million | High dividend payout ratio |

### Cash flow and balance sheet concerns

- Operating cash flow in the first half of FY2026 was US$404 million, but shareholder returns (dividends + buybacks) reached US$558 million, exceeding operating free cash flow by US$326 million
- High dividend payout ratio: earnings payout ratio 96%, operating free cash flow payout ratio 93%
- Net debt/EBITDA is approximately 2.0 times, which is still within control.
- New issuance of US$1.5 billion of senior notes in 2026

